{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.72,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["J12", "U5", "U8"],
      "pins": ["J12.A4", "U5.IN2", "U5.OUT", "U8.IN"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "VBUS"],
      "observed": "On the MCU sheet the USB-C receptacle VBUS pin J12.A4 is wired to the +5V power arrow AND also carries a red 'VBUS' global label (visible at the right edge of J12 on the MCU sheet tiles). On the power sheet U5.IN2 is the +5V net and U5.OUT is the VBUS net, so J12.A4 now joins +5V and VBUS, which also means U5.IN2 is shorted to U5.OUT.",
      "expected": "The specification states the two nets must be separate: the USB connector VBUS pin belongs to the +5V net feeding U5.IN2, while VBUS is the mux's internal pre-buck output net ('note this net is named VBUS in the netlist but is distinct from the USB +5V net upstream of the mux'). J12.A4 should carry only +5V, never VBUS.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch (top-right / right tile): J12 pin A4 connected to '+5V' arrow and labelled 'VBUS'"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "power.kicad_sch: U5 pin 2 IN2 = +5V, U5 pin 1 OUT = VBUS, U8 pin 3 IN = VBUS"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "outputs a single OUT ... onto the VBUS net (the board's internal, unregulated/pre-buck rail — note this net is named VBUS in the netlist but is distinct from the USB +5V net upstream of the mux)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The TPS2121 power mux is bypassed: the USB 5 V rail is tied directly to the mux output / buck input, and U5.IN2 is shorted to U5.OUT. Autoswitching between 12 V and USB is defeated and the 5 V and 12 V sources are effectively paralleled at the mux, risking back-feed and undefined supply behaviour.",
      "confidence": 0.8
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["U1"],
      "pins": ["U1.18", "U1.19", "U1.20", "U1.21", "U1.22"],
      "nets": ["SCK", "CS0", "CS1", "CS2"],
      "observed": "On the MCU sheet the four global labels CS0, CS1, CS2, SCK are attached to four consecutive GPIO rows of U1 in that order (CS0→GPIO03/04, CS1→GPIO04/05, CS2→GPIO05/06, SCK→GPIO06/07), so the SCK line lands on a GPIO other than GPIO05 and a chip-select lands on GPIO05; GPIO07 (a CS in firmware) is left with no label.",
      "expected": "The firmware pin map requires SCK on GPIO5 and the three chip-selects on GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7, i.e. the SCK label must sit between CS0 and CS1 (order CS0, SCK, CS1, CS2), not after CS2.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch: right side of U1 shows stacked global labels CS0, CS1, CS2, SCK on the GPIO03–GPIO06 rows"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "SPI2 configured at 60 MHz, mode 0, with sck on GPIO5 and miso on GPIO10; three independent CS Output pins on GPIO4/GPIO6/GPIO7"}
      ],
      "impact": "The SPI clock is driven on a chip-select GPIO and a chip-select on the clock GPIO, so none of the three MAX31855 thermocouple channels can be read correctly.",
      "confidence": 0.45
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Anomaly detected: the USB-C VBUS pin (J12.A4) is labelled with both +5V and VBUS, bridging the two distinct power nets the spec says must stay separate and shorting the power-mux input to its output; additionally the SPI SCK/CS global labels appear mis-assigned to the wrong ESP32 GPIO rows relative to the firmware pin map."
}

